## TODO — sidebar selection ↔ topbar (not implemented yet)

**Current behavior:** Each `*Tab.tsx` keeps its own `destination` in local `useState`. Nav rows, section headers (logical `active`), and tree rows can look or feel like multiple things are “selected” when switching rail tabs (state resets on unmount) or when both nav and section destinations were set in one tab.

**Target behavior:** One app-wide **focused view** driven by the **focused topbar tab**. The visible sidebar shows at most **one** nav/tree row with nav-style `active` highlight, derived from that focused tab when it belongs to the current rail. Section headers stay visually non-selected (header `active` override in `interactive-item.css`).

**When implementing topbar tabs:**

1. Introduce shared state (e.g. `focusedView` + `openTabs`) above `Sidebar` / `AppLayout`.
2. Replace per-tab `destination` `useState` with read/write of `focusedView`.
3. Derive sidebar `active` from `focusedView`, not independent local state.
4. Rail switch shows that rail’s sidebar; highlight only if `focusedView.workspace` matches the rail.

**Until then:** Treat local `destination` as demo-only; do not treat it as the long-term selection model.

